insert into public.questions (id, domain, objective, qtype, difficulty, stem, choices, answer, explanation) values
('q4-036',4,'4.6','mcq',2,$q$Which access control model grants permissions through job-function groups like Helpdesk-Tier1 or AP-Clerk rather than to individuals?$q$,$q$["DAC","MAC","RBAC","Rule-based"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$Role-based access control maps permissions to roles and people to roles - scalable, auditable, and the enterprise default. DAC lets owners grant ad hoc; MAC uses labels/clearances; rule-based applies condition rules (time, location).$q$),
('q4-037',4,'4.6','mcq',3,$q$A policy grants access only when: user is in Finance AND device is compliant AND location is in-country AND time is business hours. Which access control model expresses this naturally?$q$,$q$["DAC","ABAC","MAC","Standing access"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Attribute-based access control evaluates attributes of user, device, environment, and resource per request - the natural fit for contextual policies (and the engine behind Zero Trust conditional access). DAC/MAC/RBAC cannot express multi-attribute conditions cleanly.$q$),
('q4-038',4,'4.6','mcq',2,$q$Which government/military-style model enforces access via classification labels and clearances that users CANNOT override?$q$,$q$["Discretionary access control","Mandatory access control","Role-based access control","Time-of-day restrictions"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$MAC is system-enforced: resources carry labels (Secret, Top Secret), users carry clearances, and owners cannot re-grant access at will. Discretionary is owner-decided; RBAC is role-driven; time restrictions are a rule condition.$q$),
('q4-039',4,'4.6','mcq',2,$q$Which password policy element does current guidance emphasize MOST?$q$,$q$["Mandatory 30-day rotation","Length (passphrases) with breached-password screening, over complex short passwords","Requiring all four character classes in 8 characters","Monthly security questions refresh"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Modern guidance (reflected in the exam): length beats complexity, check against known-breached lists, avoid forced periodic resets without compromise indicators - frequent rotation breeds predictable patterns. Password managers enable unique long passwords.$q$),
('q4-040',4,'4.6','mcq',2,$q$Admins check out privileged credentials from a vault that rotates them after each session and records everything done. Which capability set is this?$q$,$q$["Federation","Privileged access management","NAC","Certificate pinning"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Credential vaulting, checkout, rotation, and session recording for admin accounts is PAM - controlling the highest-risk credentials. Federation delegates login; NAC gates network entry; pinning fixes expected certificates.$q$),
('q4-041',4,'4.6','mcq',3,$q$Instead of permanent admin rights, an engineer requests elevation for a two-hour change window; rights expire automatically afterward. Which TWO concepts does this implement?$q$,$q$["Just-in-time permissions and ephemeral credentials","Standing privileges and shared accounts","Password vaulting and SSO","Attestation and federation"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Granting privileges only when needed (JIT) with automatically expiring/ephemeral credentials eliminates standing admin access - nothing persistent exists to steal. The distractors describe what this replaces or unrelated mechanisms.$q$),
('q4-042',4,'4.7','mcq',2,$q$What is the PRIMARY security benefit of automating user provisioning and deprovisioning?$q$,$q$["Reduced software licensing costs","Consistent, immediate execution - no forgotten offboarding or mistyped permissions","Eliminates the need for policies","Users choose their own access"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Automation removes human lag and error from identity lifecycle: departures lose access at the moment of separation, joiners get exactly the role template. Cost savings are a side benefit; policies still define WHAT automation enforces.$q$),
('q4-043',4,'4.7','mcq',2,$q$Which are valid CONSIDERATIONS (downsides) of security automation? (Choose the BEST set.)$q$,$q$["Complexity, single point of failure, technical debt, ongoing supportability","Speed, consistency, scalability","Employee retention, reaction time","Guard rails and standard configurations"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The exam lists complexity, cost, single point of failure, technical debt, and supportability as automation risks - a broken playbook fails at machine speed. The other options list automation BENEFITS and use cases.$q$),
('q4-044',4,'4.7','mcq',2,$q$When a phishing alert fires, a playbook automatically pulls the email from all mailboxes, blocks the sender domain, isolates hosts that clicked, and opens a ticket. Which technology executes this?$q$,$q$["SIEM alone","SOAR","DLP","SCAP"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Orchestrating multiple tools through automated response playbooks is SOAR - it ACTS on what the SIEM detects. DLP guards data egress; SCAP automates configuration assessment.$q$),
('q4-045',4,'4.8','ordering',1,$q$Place the incident response phases in the correct order.$q$,$q$["Preparation","Detection","Analysis","Containment","Eradication","Recovery","Lessons learned"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3,4,5,6]$q$::jsonb,$q$Preparation happens before incidents; detection surfaces the event; analysis scopes it; containment stops the spread; eradication removes the cause; recovery restores operations; lessons learned feeds improvements back into preparation.$q$),
('q4-046',4,'4.8','mcq',2,$q$Ransomware is spreading between servers. According to the IR process, what is the IMMEDIATE priority?$q$,$q$["Lessons learned session","Containment - isolate affected segments/hosts to stop the spread","Recovery from backups","Root cause analysis"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$With an ACTIVE spreading threat, containment comes first - isolate before more systems are lost. Eradication, recovery, and analysis follow; lessons learned closes the process.$q$),
('q4-047',4,'4.8','mcq',2,$q$During which IR phase are malware removed, compromised accounts reset, and the exploited vulnerability patched?$q$,$q$["Containment","Eradication","Detection","Preparation"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Eradication removes the threat's causes and artifacts - malware, persistence, attacker accounts, and the entry vulnerability. Containment merely stops spread; recovery restores service afterward.$q$),
('q4-048',4,'4.8','mcq',2,$q$What is the purpose of a lessons-learned meeting, and when does it occur?$q$,$q$["Assign blame; during containment","Improve controls and playbooks from root causes; after recovery completes","Notify regulators; before detection","Restore backups; during eradication"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$After recovery, the blameless review documents what happened, what worked/failed, and concrete improvements - closing the loop into preparation. Blame poisons reporting; notification and restoration belong to other phases.$q$),
('q4-049',4,'4.8','mcq',2,$q$Analysts form a hypothesis that attackers may be abusing a specific admin tool and proactively search months of telemetry for matching behavior - no alert triggered this. What activity is this?$q$,$q$["Alert triage","Threat hunting","Penetration testing","Compliance audit"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Hypothesis-driven, proactive searching for compromises that evaded detection is threat hunting. Triage reacts to alerts; pen testing attacks systems; audits verify controls.$q$),
('q4-050',4,'4.8','mcq',2,$q$Which forensic practice documents every person who handled evidence, when, and what they did with it?$q$,$q$["Legal hold","Chain of custody","E-discovery","Order of volatility"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Chain of custody is the unbroken documented custody record that preserves evidence integrity and admissibility. Legal hold preserves data from deletion; e-discovery produces evidence for proceedings; order of volatility sequences collection.$q$),
('q4-051',4,'4.8','mcq',3,$q$A forensic responder arrives at a live compromised server. Per order of volatility, what is collected FIRST?$q$,$q$["Disk image","Off-site backup copies","RAM contents and running process state","Archived logs"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$Most volatile first: memory (running processes, network connections, keys) vanishes at power-off; disks persist; remote logs and backups are least volatile. Powering down before a memory capture destroys evidence.$q$),
('q4-052',4,'4.8','mcq',2,$q$Legal counsel issues a notice suspending normal data-deletion schedules for records relevant to pending litigation. What is this?$q$,$q$["Chain of custody","Legal hold","Sanitization","Right to be forgotten"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A legal hold overrides retention/deletion policies to preserve potentially relevant evidence; violating it risks sanctions. Chain of custody tracks handling; sanitization destroys data; right-to-be-forgotten is a privacy erasure right.$q$),
('q4-053',4,'4.9','mcq',2,$q$An investigation needs to determine whether a workstation reached a malicious domain yesterday. Which log source answers this MOST directly?$q$,$q$["Application debug logs","DNS query logs (or web proxy logs)","Printer logs","BIOS event logs"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$DNS logs record resolution attempts (proxy logs record the web request) - the direct evidence of contact with a domain. The other sources do not capture name resolution or web destinations.$q$),
('q4-054',4,'4.9','mcq',2,$q$Repeated Windows Event ID 4625 entries followed by a 4624 on the same account MOST likely indicate what?$q$,$q$["Normal patching","Multiple failed logons followed by a success - possible brute force that worked","Disk failure","Time sync issues"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$4625 = failed logon, 4624 = successful logon; a burst of failures ending in success is the brute-force/guessing success pattern - investigate the source and reset credentials. The other options are unrelated to authentication events.$q$),
('q4-055',4,'4.9','mcq',2,$q$Which data source shows allowed AND denied connection attempts at the network boundary, useful for spotting beaconing to command-and-control servers?$q$,$q$["Firewall logs","Application logs","OS update logs","Badge access logs"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Firewall logs record permitted/denied flows with addresses, ports, timestamps - periodic outbound connections to one external host (beaconing) stand out. App/update/badge logs cover other layers entirely.$q$),
('q4-056',4,'4.9','mcq',3,$q$Analysts have flow metadata showing a large transfer to an unknown host but need the ACTUAL CONTENT to confirm exfiltration. What must have been in place, and what is the caveat?$q$,$q$["NetFlow; it stores payloads forever","Full packet capture at that segment; encrypted payloads still need keys/TLS inspection to read","SNMP polling; it captures content","Vulnerability scanner; it replays traffic"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Only full packet capture preserves payloads, and only if capturing on that segment at that time - and TLS-encrypted content remains unreadable without decryption capability. NetFlow is metadata-only; SNMP and scanners never record traffic content.$q$),
('q4-057',4,'4.1','mcq',2,$q$A monthly scan reveals servers drifting from the hardened baseline (services re-enabled, settings changed). Which practice detects and CORRECTS this automatically?$q$,$q$["Annual audits","Configuration enforcement via management tooling (GPO/MDM/config management)","User training","Penetration testing"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Configuration enforcement continuously compares systems to the baseline and remediates drift automatically. Audits and pen tests find drift late; training does not touch configurations.$q$),
('q4-058',4,'4.5','mcq',2,$q$Which capability inspects OUTBOUND email and blocks messages containing unencrypted credit card numbers?$q$,$q$["SPF","DLP integrated with the email gateway","DKIM","Greylisting"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$DLP content inspection recognizes sensitive patterns (PANs, SSNs) in outbound mail and blocks/quarantines/encrypts per policy. SPF/DKIM authenticate senders; greylisting slows unknown senders - none inspect content for data leakage.$q$),
('q4-059',4,'4.6','mcq',2,$q$What is access ATTESTATION (recertification)?$q$,$q$["Users promising to behave","Periodic manager/owner review certifying that each user's current access is still appropriate","Biometric enrollment","Encrypting the identity database"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Attestation campaigns force owners to review and certify (or revoke) accumulated access rights on a schedule - the antidote to privilege creep from role changes. The other options are unrelated.$q$),
('q4-060',4,'4.6','mcq',2,$q$Which biometric consideration measures the rate at which UNAUTHORIZED users are incorrectly accepted?$q$,$q$["False rejection rate","False acceptance rate","Crossover error rate","Throughput rate"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$FAR counts impostors wrongly admitted (the security failure); FRR counts legitimate users wrongly denied (the usability failure); CER is where the two curves cross - the common comparison metric between systems.$q$),
('q4-061',4,'4.1','mcq',2,$q$Which mobile deployment model gives the organization the MOST control over the device?$q$,$q$["BYOD","COPE (corporate-owned, personally enabled)","Unmanaged personal devices","Guest devices"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Corporate ownership permits full management, monitoring, and wipe rights, while allowing personal use. BYOD constrains control by user ownership/privacy; unmanaged and guest devices offer no control at all.$q$),
('q4-062',4,'4.3','mcq',2,$q$Where does the Common Vulnerability Scoring System data for a specific flaw typically come from, and what does the CVE provide?$q$,$q$["CVE provides the score; CVSS provides the name","CVE provides the standardized identifier/description; CVSS provides the severity score","Both are vendor marketing terms","CVSS replaces the need for patching"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$CVE entries standardize identity (CVE-2024-XXXXX) so everyone discusses the same flaw; CVSS attaches a 0-10 severity vector to it. Together they support consistent prioritization - which still needs your exposure context.$q$),
('q4-063',4,'4.4','mcq',2,$q$What is the purpose of log ARCHIVING as distinct from alerting?$q$,$q$["Deleting logs quickly to save space","Long-term retention for investigations, compliance, and trend analysis","Real-time blocking","Compressing alerts into one email"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Archiving preserves logs beyond hot storage for forensics (attacks discovered months later), regulatory retention, and baselining. Alerting is real-time; archiving is the memory that investigations depend on.$q$),
('q4-064',4,'4.5','mcq',3,$q$A security architect proposes moving from LDAP cleartext binds on port 389 to LDAPS on 636 for directory authentication. What threat does this SPECIFICALLY mitigate?$q$,$q$["SQL injection against the directory","Credential capture by network sniffing of bind operations","Phishing of user passwords","Brute force against accounts"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Cleartext LDAP binds expose credentials to anyone on-path; LDAPS wraps the session in TLS so sniffing yields ciphertext. It does nothing against phishing, guessing, or injection - different controls cover those.$q$),
('q4-065',4,'4.8','mcq',2,$q$What is the goal of ROOT CAUSE ANALYSIS after an incident?$q$,$q$["Identify which analyst responded slowest","Determine the underlying cause so the fix prevents recurrence, not just the symptom","Calculate insurance premiums","Assign legal liability"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$RCA digs past symptoms (malware on host) to causes (unpatched VPN, missing MFA, no segmentation) so remediation prevents the CLASS of incident. Blame-seeking suppresses honest reporting and is explicitly not the goal.$q$),
('q4-066',4,'4.8','mcq',2,$q$A phishing SIMULATION is run quarterly and results feed targeted training. Which IR phase does this activity belong to?$q$,$q$["Containment","Preparation","Eradication","Recovery"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Testing and training people before incidents - simulations, tabletops, playbook drills - is preparation. The exercise builds the detection/reporting muscle the real incident will need.$q$),
('q4-067',4,'4.9','mcq',2,$q$Which combination of sources BEST reconstructs what a compromised endpoint did? (Choose the BEST set.)$q$,$q$["Endpoint/EDR logs + firewall logs + DNS logs","Badge logs + HVAC telemetry","Printer queue + wallpaper settings","Marketing analytics + CRM exports"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$EDR shows local execution (processes, files, persistence), firewall logs show network conversations, DNS shows lookups - together mapping local actions to external contacts. The other sets are irrelevant to endpoint forensics.$q$),
('q4-068',4,'4.6','mcq',3,$q$A company implements passkeys (FIDO2) replacing passwords. Why is this MORE phishing-resistant than password+TOTP?$q$,$q$["Passkeys are longer passwords","The credential is origin-bound cryptography - it cannot be typed into or replayed from a fake site, while TOTP codes can be phished in real time","TOTP requires no user interaction","Passkeys expire monthly"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$FIDO2 signs a challenge bound to the legitimate site's origin, so a look-alike site receives nothing reusable; users CAN be tricked into typing TOTP codes into proxies that replay them instantly. Passkeys are keys, not passwords, and do not expire monthly.$q$),
('q4-069',4,'4.7','mcq',2,$q$Development pipelines run automated security tests on every commit, blocking merges that fail. Which automation use case is this?$q$,$q$["Ticket creation","Continuous integration and testing (guard rails)","User provisioning","Disabling access"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Security gates in CI are the exam's continuous integration/testing use case - automation acting as guard rails so insecure changes cannot ship. The other use cases automate identity and workflow tasks.$q$),
('q4-070',4,'4.4','mcq',2,$q$After a monitoring alert proves valid and the affected host is quarantined, which follow-up step VALIDATES the response?$q$,$q$["Delete the alert","Confirm remediation via rescan/verification and document the outcome","Disable monitoring on that host","Email the CEO hourly"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The monitoring workflow ends with remediation validation and documentation - proving the threat is gone and the response worked (and tuning rules if it was noisy). Deleting alerts or blinding monitoring destroys the record and the visibility.$q$);
